 # fieldwise
 
-Schema-driven document extraction with a review UI and per-field evals: define a schema, drop in
-PDFs or scans, get structured data, review it next to the page, and measure accuracy across prompt
-versions.
+**Schema-driven document extraction with a review UI and per-field evals.** Define a schema, drop
+in PDFs or receipt photos, get structured data from a vision model, review it next to the page, and
+measure per-field accuracy across prompt versions — on a public dataset, with reproducible numbers.
 
-Work in progress — assembled in small pull requests; the README grows with the measured results.
+[![CI](https://github.com/ilia-muravev-dev/fieldwise/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/ilia-muravev-dev/fieldwise/actions/workflows/ci.yml)
+[![license](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-blue)](./LICENSE)
+
+![Review page: the extracted total highlighted on the receipt it was read from](docs/images/review.png)
+
+## Why this exists
+
+I ran a document pipeline in production — scans and PDFs in, OCR, an LLM extracting structured
+fields, a review UI for the humans, about 40k documents a month. The number that mattered was
+field accuracy on a fixed golden set, and it only moved when it was measured the same way after
+every change: 81% → 93% over four iterations of prompts and schemas. This repository is that
+loop, rebuilt in the open on receipts instead of invoices:
+
+- **Evals first.** Every prompt version is a pull request with its measured delta. The grader is
+  checked by construction (an oracle scores 100%, a null baseline 0% value accuracy) and CI re-scores
+  recorded model responses on every push — for free.
+- **Structured outputs, not regexes.** The authored JSON Schema becomes the model's output schema,
+  the grader's field list and the UI's tree, from one file.
+- **Reviewers close the loop.** A correction saved in the UI becomes the document's golden label and
+  a few-shot example for similar documents.
+
+## Results so far
+
+Measured on the [CORD v2](https://huggingface.co/datasets/naver-clova-ix/cord-v2) test split
+(Indonesian receipts, CC BY 4.0), 20 documents, OpenRouter's free `nex-agi/nex-n2.5-pro`, effort low.
+Every number below is copied from a report in [`docs/evals/`](docs/evals/) written by the eval runner.
+
+| Metric | v1 — schema + image | v2 — + OCR text, normalisation rules |
+| --- | ---: | ---: |
+| Field accuracy (strict, every leaf) | 84.5% | **91.1%** |
+| Value accuracy (leaves with a golden value) | 86.3% | **95.1%** |
+| Documents fully correct | 36.8% | **50.0%** |
+| `line_items` exact match | 42.1% | **66.7%** |
+| `total` | 78.9% | 94.4% |
+| `line_items[].name` | 73.9% | 81.6% |
+| Not scored (truncated / rate-limited) | 1 / 0 | 0 / 2 |
+
+What v1 got wrong, from the per-document error listing: thousands separators read as decimals
+(`"60.000"` → `60`), sub-items (toppings, add-ons) promoted to line items, and item names
+"corrected" instead of copied. v2 spells those rules out and adds the OCR text next to the image.
+The same analysis also caught a bug on the *golden* side — `"Rp. 111,000"` parsed as `111.0` — which
+was fixed, the labels re-mapped, and v1 re-scored from its recordings.
+
+Prompt **v3** (three retrieved few-shot examples) and **v4** (evidence quotes, a self-check that the
+line totals add up, one repair round) are implemented and registered; their measurements, the
+ablations (no few-shot, image only, effort) and the 100-document runs are the next thing this README
+gets. The free tier of the model above allows ~50 requests a day, which is what set the pace.
+
+![Comparison page: fields as rows, prompt versions as columns](docs/images/compare.png)
+
+## How it works
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart LR
+    U[Upload / CORD import] --> R[Render pages<br/>pypdfium2 · Pillow]
+    R --> O[OCR<br/>RapidOCR: lines, words, boxes]
+    O --> E[Embed OCR text<br/>fastembed · pgvector]
+    E --> F[Few-shot retrieval<br/>labelled, never test split]
+    F --> M[Vision model, structured output<br/>Claude · OpenRouter · Ollama]
+    M --> V[Validate · consistency check<br/>one repair round]
+    V --> G[Ground values in OCR<br/>confidence + boxes]
+    G --> S[(ExtractionRun<br/>tokens · cost · latency)]
+    S --> UI[Review UI] & EV[Eval runner]
+```
+
+- **Schemas** ([`schemas/compiler.py`](backend/src/fieldwise/schemas/compiler.py)): an authored
+  JSON Schema is compiled into the strict schema structured outputs need (closed objects, every
+  property required, every leaf nullable) plus per-field metadata with a matcher (`money`,
+  `integer`, `text`, `list`).
+- **Providers** ([`extraction/provider.py`](backend/src/fieldwise/extraction/provider.py),
+  [`openai_compat.py`](backend/src/fieldwise/extraction/openai_compat.py)): the Anthropic SDK
+  (cached system block, `output_config` with effort and a JSON-schema format, Batches API) and any
+  OpenAI-compatible endpoint (OpenRouter, a local Ollama, OpenAI) behind one protocol; a fake
+  provider for tests and a cassette provider that records and replays.
+- **Grading** ([`evals/matchers.py`](backend/src/fieldwise/evals/matchers.py), ADR 0003): six
+  outcomes per leaf, lists aligned by position, money compared as decimals after the same
+  normalisation the importer applied, infrastructure failures in a sidecar and never in a denominator.
+- **Review UI** (`web/`, Next.js 16): the page image with OCR boxes, the field tree with confidence,
+  the golden label side by side, corrections, run stats, eval comparison.
+
+## Quickstart
+
+```bash
+git clone https://github.com/ilia-muravev-dev/fieldwise && cd fieldwise
+cp backend/.env.example backend/.env      # choose a provider below
+just app                                   # Postgres+pgvector, Redis, migrations, API :8000, worker, web :3000
+```
+
+Pick a model provider in `backend/.env`:
+
+| Provider | Settings | Notes |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Anthropic | `LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=…`, `DEFAULT_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5` | structured outputs, prompt caching, `--batch` at half price |
+| OpenRouter | `LLM_PROVIDER=openai`, `OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1`, `OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-or-…`, `DEFAULT_MODEL=nex-agi/nex-n2.5-pro:free` | free vision models exist; ~50 requests/day without credits |
+| Ollama (local) | `LLM_PROVIDER=openai`, `OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1` (`http://host.docker.internal:11434/v1` from Docker), `OPENAI_API_KEY=ollama`, `DEFAULT_MODEL=qwen2.5vl:7b` | free and offline; slower |
+| none | `LLM_PROVIDER=fake` | the stack runs, extractions return nulls (what the e2e tests use) |
+
+Then, from `backend/`:
+
+```bash
+uv sync
+uv run fieldwise ingest cord --split test --split validation   # 200 labelled receipts (~480 MB download)
+uv run fieldwise ocr run                                        # RapidOCR, ~1 minute on a laptop
+uv run fieldwise embed                                          # bge-small embeddings for few-shot retrieval
+uv run fieldwise extract cord-v2/test/0000 --prompt v2          # one document, with tokens, cost, latency
+uv run fieldwise eval run --prompt v2 --limit 20 --cassette record   # a measurement; asks before spending
+uv run fieldwise eval compare <run-a> <run-b> --write           # the table above
+```
+
+Open http://localhost:3000 for the review UI. Upload your own PDF or photo there; the worker runs OCR,
+and "Run extraction" queues a model call you can watch land.
+
+## The eval loop, precisely
+
+1. `fieldwise eval run` prepares every request first, then executes them in a thread pool, one by
+   one, or as one Message Batch; a second phase runs the v4 repair turns.
+2. Each extraction is graded against the document's golden label — a reviewer's correction wins
+   over the dataset label. Failed and truncated runs go to the sidecar with their cause.
+3. Two headline numbers are always shown together: **strict** field accuracy (null agreements
+   count) and **value** accuracy (only leaves with a golden value), plus the document exact-match
+   rate, Wilson intervals per field, cost from the API's usage block, latency p50/p95.
+4. Reports land in `docs/evals/` as Markdown and JSON; `eval compare` renders the README table.
+5. CI (`eval-gate`) loads a committed bundle of 20 test receipts into a fresh database, replays the
+   recorded responses, re-scores them and fails on a > 2-point regression of any field.
+
+Few-shot examples come from labelled non-test documents only; the exclusion is in the SQL
+(ADR 0004), and a test asserts a test document's identical twin is never retrieved.
+
+## Repository
+
+```
+backend/   Python 3.12 · FastAPI · SQLAlchemy 2 · Celery · Typer CLI · RapidOCR · fastembed · pgvector
+  src/fieldwise/{schemas,documents,extraction,retrieval,evals,api,worker,gate}
+  evals/cassettes/   recorded model responses      evals/gate/   the CI fixture bundle
+web/       Next.js 16 · React 19 · Tailwind 4 · shadcn · TanStack Query · typed client from openapi.json
+docs/adr/  decisions      docs/evals/  measurements      openapi.json  the API contract (checked in CI)
+```
+
+## Decisions
+
+| ADR | Decision |
+| --- | --- |
+| [0001](docs/adr/0001-evals-before-ui.md) | The eval harness comes before the UI; README numbers are copied from its reports |
+| [0002](docs/adr/0002-rapidocr-over-tesseract.md) | RapidOCR (PP-OCR on ONNX, pip-only) over Tesseract |
+| [0003](docs/adr/0003-how-extractions-are-graded.md) | Six outcomes per leaf; strict and value accuracy side by side; failures never scored |
+| [0004](docs/adr/0004-few-shot-pool-excludes-the-test-split.md) | The few-shot pool structurally excludes the test split |
+| [0005](docs/adr/0005-recorded-responses-make-ci-free.md) | Recorded responses make the CI gate free and deterministic |
+
+## Development
+
+```bash
+just check        # backend: ruff, mypy --strict, pytest (unit + pgvector testcontainer); openapi freshness; web: biome, tsc, vitest, build
+just app-e2e      # the stack with the fake provider
+just e2e          # Playwright: upload → OCR → extraction → correction
+just openapi      # regenerate openapi.json and the typed web client
+```
+
+Dataset: CORD (Park et al., 2019), CC BY 4.0; three downscaled receipts live in
